    11	---
    12	
    13	## The problem, in three lines
    14	
    15	- The model was trained on **`assert`** — "did this road user take the gap?" — because `yield` occurs
    16	  1 in 301 in METEOR and cannot be learned. That decision was correct and is documented in
    17	  `ml/ReadThis.md`.
    18	- The contract field is called **`PYield`**, the export is `yield_lstm_opset<N>.onnx`, and its
    19	  output tensor is `yield_logits`.
    20	- **`ml/ReadThis.md` says explicitly: "Not asserting is not the same as yielding."** So it is not
    21	  even a clean `1 - p` flip. A driver who simply carries on doing nothing is in the negative class
    22	  for both.
    23	
    24	Plug P(assert) into a field named `PYield` and **D6 weights its two futures backwards**, silently,
    25	with nothing erroring. That is the exact failure mode this project keeps losing to.
    26	
    27	---
    28	
    29	## The ruling
    30	
    31	> **`PYield` carries `1 - P(assert)`: the probability that the other road user does NOT take the gap.**
    32	
    33	Stream C computes it. Stream D consumes it. Nobody renames anything, and section 3 stays frozen.
    34	
    35	### Why this and not the alternatives
    36	
    37	**Not `PYield = P(assert)`.** The field name would then mean the opposite of what it says. Someone
    38	reads `PYield` in six weeks, assumes it means yield, and the planner drives into the gap.
    39	
    40	**Not "`Valid = false` always, ignore the model."** Safe, but it throws away the entire ML stream
    41	and there is no reason to — see the safety argument below.
    42	
    43	### The honest caveat, which must go in the report
    44	
    45	`1 - P(assert)` lumps **"actively yielded"** together with **"did nothing"**. Those are not the same
    46	thing, and treating "did nothing" as yielding is **optimistic**. State it plainly:
    47	
    48	> We predict whether the other road user will take the gap. Not taking it is what our safety check
    49	> actually needs to know. We do not claim to predict active yielding.
    50	
    51	That is Aditya's wording from `ml/ReadThis.md`, and it is already the agreed language.
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	## Why an optimistic `PYield` is not a safety hole
    56	
    57	This only holds because of the D6 ruling, so it must not be separated from it.
    58	
    59	**`PYield` does not decide what is safe. It decides what is preferred.**
    60	
    61	Per `plan/D6-TRUNK-RULING.md`, the trunk is the prefix that is safe **under both futures** — yield
    62	*and* assert — and a braking-to-stop must be clear from the end of it under both. The weights never
    63	enter that test. They only rank candidates that have already passed it.
    64	
    65	**So a mis-weighted `PYield` costs efficiency, not safety.** The car dithers or waits longer than it
    66	needed to. It does not drive into anything. The barrier `h = lambda - beta` and the both-futures
    67	check are what carry safety, and neither reads `PYield`.
    68	
    69	**Stream D: if you ever find yourself using `PYield` to decide whether something is safe rather than
    70	which safe thing to prefer, stop. That is outside this ruling and the argument above no longer
    71	holds.**
