One Indian finding changes the prior design: a seven-class junction study reports smaller accepted gaps for two-wheelers than for buses and trucks, but that measures a vehicle’s own gap-taking decision. It does not justify assigning “selfishness” or yielding probabilities by class. I’ll use those figures only for maneuver priors and keep responsiveness to ego as a separately measured quantity.
